---
import LessonLayout from '../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
---

<LessonLayout
  title='Extension & scope functions'
  description='Extension functions as the engine, then let, apply, run, also, with on a 2×2: who is this and what does the block return.'
  number=4
  primary='#2f6f5e'
  accent='#5b6cad'
>
  <div class="eyebrow">Kotlin · Context & scope</div>
    <h1>Extension & scope functions, as <em>one</em> toolkit.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <code>let</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>also</code>, <code>with</code>, and
      extension functions look like six separate features. They're really six
      dials on the same question: <strong>who is <code>this</code> right now,
      what do I call the value, and what does the block return?</strong>
    </p>

    <div class="meta">
      <span>⏱ ~10 min read</span>
      <span>🎯 Early intermediate</span>
      <span>🧠 Mental model first, syntax second</span>
    </div>

    <!-- THE BIG QUESTION -->
    <div class="question">
      <div class="question-label">The question behind all six</div>
      <p class="question-text">
        Inside any block of code, three things are decided for you by the language:
        <br><br>
        1. <strong>What is <code>this</code>?</strong><br>
        2. <strong>What's the name of the value I have in scope?</strong><br>
        3. <strong>What does the block evaluate to?</strong>
        <br><br>
        Every tool on this page just makes one of those three things explicit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- SCOPE FUNCTIONS 2x2 -->
    <h2>The five scope functions, on one map</h2>
    <p>
      Forget the names for a moment. Look at the structure. Five functions,
      <strong>two axes</strong>:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>What is <code>this</code>?</strong> — your value is named <code>it</code> (you pass it in like an arg) or it's the implicit <code>this</code> (you're inside its methods).</li>
      <li><strong>What does the block return?</strong> — the receiver itself, or the last expression of the block.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      That gives you a 2×2 grid. The fifth one, <code>with</code>, is just
      "the function-form of <code>run</code>". Everything else snaps into place.
    </p>

    <div class="matrix-wrap">
      <div class="matrix-title">Scope functions · the 2×2</div>
      <div class="matrix">
        <div class="matrix-corner"></div>
        <div class="matrix-col-head">Block returns: result</div>
        <div class="matrix-col-head">Block returns: receiver</div>

        <div class="matrix-row-head">Ref: <code>this</code></div>
        <div class="matrix-cell q1">
          <div class="fn-name">run</div>
          <div class="fn-sub">"compute something on this"</div>
          <div class="fn-ref">Ref: <span class="r-this">this</span></div>
          <div class="fn-ret">Returns: <code>last expression</code></div>
        </div>
        <div class="matrix-cell q2">
          <div class="fn-name">apply</div>
          <div class="fn-sub">"configure this, then keep it"</div>
          <div class="fn-ref">Ref: <span class="r-this">this</span></div>
          <div class="fn-ret">Returns: <code>the receiver</code></div>
        </div>

        <div class="matrix-row-head">Ref: <code>it</code></div>
        <div class="matrix-cell q3">
          <div class="fn-name">let</div>
          <div class="fn-sub">"transform, or null-check"</div>
          <div class="fn-ref">Ref: <span class="r-it">it</span></div>
          <div class="fn-ret">Returns: <code>last expression</code></div>
        </div>
        <div class="matrix-cell q4">
          <div class="fn-name">also</div>
          <div class="fn-sub">"side-effect, keep the original"</div>
          <div class="fn-ref">Ref: <span class="r-it">it</span></div>
          <div class="fn-ret">Returns: <code>the receiver</code></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>Read it like this:</strong> "I want to <em>compute</em> something" → left column.
      "I want to <em>keep the original</em>" → right column. "I want to call methods on it
      without saying the name" → top row (<code>this</code>). "I want to pass it like an arg
      and maybe give it a custom name" → bottom row (<code>it</code>).
    </div>

    <!-- EXTENSION FUNCTIONS -->
    <h2><span class="num">E</span>Extension functions — "add a method without owning the class"</h2>

    <p>
      Before the scope functions, you need to understand what an extension function is.
      It's the building block they all rest on, because <em>every</em> scope function
      is essentially an extension function with a fixed shape.
    </p>

    <p>
      An extension function says: "for this type, let me call this function the way I'd
      call a method." It doesn't actually modify the class. The compiler just translates
      <code>value.greet()</code> into <code>greet(value)</code> at the call site.
    </p>

    <div class="concept" data-t="ext">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14 7h6v6"/><path d="M10 17 21 6"/><path d="M18 18 6 6l-3 3 12 12 3-3z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">fun ReceiverType.fnName(...): ReturnType</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">fun &lt;Type&gt;.&lt;name&gt;() — you don't need the source.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        You write a function as if it were a method on a type. Inside, <code>this</code>
        is the value you called it on. That's it.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// Without touching the String class, we can add a method.</span>
  <span class="k">fun</span> <span class="t">String</span>.<span class="fn">greet</span>(): <span class="t">String</span> = <span class="s">"Hello, </span><span class="th">$this</span><span class="s">!"</span>

  <span class="k">val</span> msg = <span class="s">"world"</span>.<span class="fn">greet</span>()   <span class="c">// → "Hello, world!"</span>

  <span class="c">// Inside the body, `this` is the value — same idea as a class method.</span></div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>The receiver <code>this</code>:</strong> the value before the dot becomes
        <code>this</code> inside the function. That's why scope functions work — they
        all use the same mechanism.
      </div>

      <h3>Two things extensions unlock that you can't do with a normal function</h3>

      <div class="pair">
        <div>
          <div class="pair-label neutral">Nullable receivers</div>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">fun</span> <span class="t">String</span>?.<span class="fn">orDash</span>(): <span class="t">String</span> =
      <span class="th">this</span> ?: <span class="s">"—"</span>

  <span class="c">// Now any String? has orDash(), no null-check needed</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> a: <span class="t">String</span>? = <span class="k">null</span>
  a.<span class="fn">orDash</span>()   <span class="c">// "—", no NPE</span></div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="pair-label neutral">Member-like call sites</div>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">fun</span> <span class="t">List</span>&lt;<span class="t">Int</span>&gt;.<span class="fn">secondOrNull</span>(): <span class="t">Int</span>? =
      <span class="k">if</span> (<span class="th">this</span>.size &gt;= <span class="n">2</span>) <span class="th">this</span>[<span class="n">1</span>] <span class="k">else</span> <span class="k">null</span>

  <span class="k">val</span> xs = <span class="fn">listOf</span>(<span class="n">1</span>, <span class="n">2</span>, <span class="n">3</span>)
  xs.<span class="fn">secondOrNull</span>()   <span class="c">// 2</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>Why scope functions matter now:</strong> every scope function
      (<code>let</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>also</code>) is an
      <em>extension function</em> on a generic receiver. So when you read
      <code>value.let &#123; ... &#125;</code>, you're really calling
      <code>let(value) &#123; ... &#125;</code> — and inside the block, <code>value</code>
      is bound to <code>it</code> (or <code>this</code>, depending on which one).
      <strong>Extension functions are the engine. Scope functions are the dashboard.</strong>
    </div>

    <!-- SCOPE: LET -->
    <h2><span class="num">L</span>let — "give this value a name, then maybe transform it"</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="let">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">let</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">receiver → block; ref = it; returns last expression</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        The value arrives as <code>it</code>. You run a block. The block's result is what
        <code>let</code> returns. Two superpowers: <strong>null-check</strong> and
        <strong>transform-into-something-else</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// 1. Null-check — block only runs if value is non-null</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> email: <span class="t">String</span>? = <span class="fn">readEmail</span>()
  <span class="k">val</span> name = email?.<span class="fn">let</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.substringBefore(<span class="s">"@"</span>) &#125;
  <span class="c">//        ↑ safe call: block runs only if email != null</span>

  <span class="c">// 2. Transform — turn one value into another</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> length = <span class="s">"hello"</span>.<span class="fn">let</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.length &#125;   <span class="c">// 5</span>

  <span class="c">// 3. Rename it for clarity</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> pretty = <span class="s">"hi"</span>.<span class="fn">let</span> &#123; msg -&gt; <span class="s">"Message: </span><span class="th">$msg</span><span class="s">"</span> &#125;</div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> if you wrote <code>?.let &#123; &#125;</code>, you wanted a
        null-check. If you wrote <code>.let &#123; &#125;</code> without the <code>?</code>, you
        wanted a transform or a local name.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SCOPE: APPLY -->
    <h2><span class="num">A</span>apply — "configure this object, then return it"</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="apply">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 20h9"/><path d="M16.5 3.5a2.121 2.121 0 0 1 3 3L7 19l-4 1 1-4L16.5 3.5z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">apply</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">receiver → block; ref = this; returns the receiver</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        You step <em>into</em> the object. Inside the block, you can call its methods
        without naming it — they're all in scope. When you're done, the object comes back
        out, fully configured.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// The classic "build an Intent" example</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> intent = <span class="t">Intent</span>(<span class="t">Intent</span>.<span class="v">ACTION_VIEW</span>).<span class="fn">apply</span> &#123;
      <span class="th">data</span> = <span class="t">Uri</span>.<span class="fn">parse</span>(<span class="s">"https://kotlinlang.org"</span>)
      <span class="th">putExtra</span>(<span class="s">"user_id"</span>, <span class="n">42</span>)
      <span class="th">addFlags</span>(<span class="t">Intent</span>.<span class="v">FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK</span>)
  &#125;
  <span class="c">// intent is the same Intent object, just configured. All three setters ran on `this`.</span></div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>When to use it:</strong> anytime you're setting 2+ properties on a fresh
        object and then passing it along. <code>apply</code> is the "builder pattern, but free."
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SCOPE: RUN -->
    <h2><span class="num">R</span>run — "do a computation on this, give me the answer"</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="run">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polygon points="6 3 20 12 6 21 6 3"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">run</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">receiver → block; ref = this; returns last expression</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        Looks like <code>apply</code> (you're inside <code>this</code>), but the value
        coming out is the block's <em>result</em>, not the object itself. Use it when
        you want to ask the object a question or compute something from it.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// 1. Compute something on an object</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> area = <span class="t">Rect</span>(width = <span class="n">3</span>, height = <span class="n">4</span>).<span class="fn">run</span> &#123;
      <span class="th">width</span> * <span class="th">height</span>
  &#125;   <span class="c">// 12, not the Rect</span>

  <span class="c">// 2. As a no-receiver block — just a scoped statement block</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> config = <span class="fn">run</span> &#123;
      <span class="k">val</span> env = <span class="fn">loadEnv</span>()
      <span class="k">val</span> defaults = <span class="t">Defaults</span>()
      defaults.<span class="fn">merge</span>(env)
  &#125;</div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>Mnemonic:</strong> "<code>run</code> this code, give me the answer." The
        receiver is a convenience for reading. The return is the point.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SCOPE: ALSO -->
    <h2><span class="num">A</span>also — "do a side-effect, then keep going"</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="also">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 12a9 9 0 1 0 9-9 9.75 9.75 0 0 0-6.74 2.74L3 8"/><path d="M3 3v5h5"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">also</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">receiver → block; ref = it; returns the receiver</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        The value arrives as <code>it</code>. You do something with it (log it, validate
        it, save it somewhere). The <em>same value</em> comes out the other side.
        This is the scope function for <strong>side effects</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// Classic: log a value, then return it unchanged</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> user = <span class="fn">loadUser</span>(id).<span class="fn">also</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span> -&gt;
      <span class="t">Logger</span>.<span class="fn">d</span>(<span class="s">"loaded user </span><span class="th">$&#123;</span><span class="it">it</span>.<span class="th">id</span><span class="th">&#125;</span><span class="s">"</span>)
  &#125;

  <span class="c">// Chain: do the side-effect, then keep using the same object</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> saved = user.<span class="fn">validate</span>().<span class="fn">also</span> &#123; <span class="fn">repo</span>.<span class="fn">save</span>(<span class="it">it</span>) &#125;

  <span class="c">// Without `also`, the chain would lose the object to repo.save().</span></div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>The key signal you want <code>also</code>:</strong> "I need to do
        <em>something else</em> with this value, but I still need the value afterward."
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SCOPE: WITH -->
    <h2><span class="num">W</span>with — "call a bunch of methods on this object"</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="with">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><line x1="3" y1="9" x2="21" y2="9"/><line x1="9" y1="21" x2="9" y2="9"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">with(obj) &#123; ... &#125;</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">same as run, but called as a function — obj is the first arg</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        Function form of <code>run</code>: you call <code>with(file) &#123; ... &#125;</code>
        and inside, <code>this</code> is <code>file</code>. It returns the last expression.
        Use it when you have an existing object and want to run several operations on it
        in a row.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> firstLines = <span class="fn">with</span>(<span class="t">File</span>(<span class="s">"README.md"</span>)) &#123;
      <span class="k">if</span> (!<span class="th">exists</span>()) <span class="k">return@with</span> <span class="t">emptyList</span>&lt;<span class="t">String</span>&gt;()
      <span class="th">readLines</span>().<span class="fn">take</span>(<span class="n">5</span>)
  &#125;</div>

      <div class="pair">
        <div>
          <div class="pair-label good">run form</div>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> firstLines = <span class="t">File</span>(<span class="s">"README.md"</span>).<span class="fn">run</span> &#123;
      <span class="k">if</span> (!<span class="th">exists</span>()) <span class="k">return@run</span> <span class="t">emptyList</span>&lt;<span class="t">String</span>&gt;()
      <span class="th">readLines</span>().<span class="fn">take</span>(<span class="n">5</span>)
  &#125;</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="pair-label neutral">with form (same effect)</div>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> firstLines = <span class="fn">with</span>(<span class="t">File</span>(<span class="s">"README.md"</span>)) &#123;
      <span class="k">if</span> (!<span class="th">exists</span>()) <span class="k">return@with</span> <span class="t">emptyList</span>&lt;<span class="t">String</span>&gt;()
      <span class="th">readLines</span>().<span class="fn">take</span>(<span class="n">5</span>)
  &#125;</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>Style note:</strong> <code>run</code> reads like a method call, <code>with</code>
        reads like a statement. Pick whichever feels more natural at the call site. Same power.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- WHERE "this" LIVES -->
    <h2>Where <code>this</code> actually lives</h2>
    <p>
      The reason the 2×2 is a real thing and not a mnemonic trick: extension functions
      literally rebind <code>this</code> to a new value for the duration of the block.
      That makes them nestable — and the nesting is the part that confuses people.
    </p>

    <div class="scope-diagram">
      <h3>Nested scope — what <code>this</code> points to at each layer</h3>

      <div class="scope-row">
        <div class="scope-box outer">
          <div class="scope-tag">OUTER</div>
          <p>Class scope. <code>this</code> = the class instance.</p>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">fun</span> <span class="fn">render</span>(order: <span class="t">Order</span>) &#123;
      <span class="c">// `this` = the enclosing class (let's say, a Presenter)</span>
      order.<span class="fn">apply</span> &#123;
          <span class="c">// inside apply: `this` = the Order. No name needed.</span>
          <span class="th">items</span>.<span class="fn">forEach</span> &#123; item -&gt;
              <span class="c">// inside forEach lambda: `this` is STILL the Order.</span>
              <span class="c">// `it` (or `item`) is each element.</span>
              <span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="s">"</span><span class="th">$</span><span class="th">this</span><span class="s">.id: </span><span class="th">$item</span><span class="s">"</span>)
          &#125;
      &#125;
  &#125;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="scope-box inner">
          <div class="scope-tag">TAKEAWAY</div>
          <p>
            The innermost <code>this</code> always wins. If you need the outer one,
            capture it: <code>val outer = this</code> before the inner block.
          </p>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> outer = <span class="th">this</span>   <span class="c">// capture</span>
  order.<span class="fn">apply</span> &#123;
      <span class="c">// now both `this` (Order) and `outer` (class) are reachable</span>
  &#125;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION TREE -->
    <h2>Picking one: a 30-second decision tree</h2>
    <p>
      You don't have to memorize the matrix. Walk through this:
    </p>

    <div class="tree">
      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q1 · I need the value to come out the other side unchanged?</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">If yes → you want <code>apply</code> (using methods on it) or <code>also</code> (passing it somewhere as a side-effect).</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ apply / also (right column)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q2 · I need to compute something FROM the value?</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">If yes → you want the block's last expression. <code>run</code> if you'll read its members; <code>let</code> if you'll pass it as a value (esp. for null-checks).</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ run / let (left column)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q3 · Will I call a method on it, or treat it as a value?</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">
          Call a method: <code>this</code> form → <code>apply</code> or <code>run</code>.
          Treat as a value (transform / log / pass somewhere): <code>it</code> form → <code>let</code> or <code>also</code>.
        </div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ this row vs it row</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- UNIFYING DIAGRAM -->
    <div class="unify">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0; font-size: 28px;">The one-idea view: scope functions = extension functions, inline</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--ink-mid); font-size: 15px;">
        An extension function is a <em>permanent</em> change to how a type behaves —
        you write it once, you call it forever. A scope function is the same thing
        but <em>one-shot</em>: you borrow the same machinery (<code>this</code>-as-receiver,
        a block) for a single expression.
      </p>

      <div class="unify-row">
        <div class="unify-card">
          <div class="unify-card-label">Extension function</div>
          <h4>Permanent, named</h4>
          <p>You write <code>fun String.greet()</code> once. Every String has it.</p>
          <div class="code" style="margin-top:12px; font-size:12.5px;"><span class="k">fun</span> <span class="t">String</span>.<span class="fn">greet</span>() = <span class="s">"Hi </span><span class="th">$this</span><span class="s">"</span>
  <span class="c">// call site:</span>
  <span class="s">"Mavis"</span>.<span class="fn">greet</span>()   <span class="c">// "Hi Mavis"</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="arrow">
          <svg width="40" height="40" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h14"/><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="unify-card">
          <div class="unify-card-label">Scope function</div>
          <h4>One-shot, inline</h4>
          <p>The same mechanism, but you don't name it. You borrow the receiver for one block.</p>
          <div class="code" style="margin-top:12px; font-size:12.5px;"><span class="k">val</span> msg = <span class="s">"Mavis"</span>.<span class="fn">let</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.<span class="fn">uppercase</span>() &#125;
  <span class="c">// `it` is `this` for the duration of the block</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- RECIPES -->
    <h2>Common recipes (copy these)</h2>

    <div class="recipes">
      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Null-check with let</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Run code only if the value isn't null, and probably return something.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> display = email?.<span class="fn">let</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.<span class="fn">substringBefore</span>(<span class="s">"@"</span>) &#125;
  <span class="c">// → null if email is null, else the local part</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Build &amp; return with apply</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Set fields on a fresh object and pass it along in one expression.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> req = <span class="t">Request</span>().<span class="fn">apply</span> &#123;
      <span class="th">url</span> = <span class="s">"/users"</span>
      <span class="th">method</span> = <span class="s">"GET"</span>
      <span class="th">timeout</span> = <span class="n">5_000</span>
  &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Log with also</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Tap into a chain to log without breaking it.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> user = <span class="fn">load</span>().<span class="fn">also</span> &#123; <span class="t">Log</span>.<span class="fn">d</span>(<span class="s">"u="</span> + <span class="it">it</span>.<span class="th">id</span>) &#125;
      .<span class="fn">validate</span>()
      .<span class="fn">save</span>()</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Compute with run</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Read a few fields of an object to produce something else.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> area = rect.<span class="fn">run</span> &#123;
      <span class="th">width</span> * <span class="th">height</span>   <span class="c">// returns Int</span>
  &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Many calls with with</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Group a sequence of operations on a single object.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> result = <span class="fn">with</span>(<span class="t">Bitmap</span>.<span class="fn">createBitmap</span>(w, h, cfg)) &#123;
      <span class="th">eraseColor</span>(<span class="n">0</span>)
      <span class="th">setPixel</span>(...)
      <span class="fn">compressToJpeg</span>()
  &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Custom extension → method</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">When a scope function would repeat, promote it to an extension.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">fun</span> <span class="t">String</span>?.<span class="fn">orEmpty</span>() = <span class="th">this</span> ?: <span class="s">""</span>
  <span class="c">// (stdlib already has this, but you see the shape.)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- RECAP -->
    <div class="recap">
      <h2><span class="num">↺</span>Recap in one minute</h2>
      <p>
        Six features, one question: <em>who is <code>this</code>, what name does my
        value have, and what does the block return?</em>
      </p>
      <div class="recap-grid">
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">fun T.name()</div>
          <div class="desc">Extension function. Add a method to any type, even ones you don't own. The receiver becomes <code>this</code> inside.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">let</div>
          <div class="desc">Receiver as <code>it</code>, returns block result. <strong>Use for null-checks and transforms.</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">apply</div>
          <div class="desc">Receiver as <code>this</code>, returns receiver. <strong>Use for "configure-and-keep".</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">run</div>
          <div class="desc">Receiver as <code>this</code>, returns block result. <strong>Use for "compute on this".</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">also</div>
          <div class="desc">Receiver as <code>it</code>, returns receiver. <strong>Use for side effects (log, validate, save).</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">with(obj) &#123; ... &#125;</div>
          <div class="desc">Function form of <code>run</code>. <strong>Use for "do many things on this object".</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="end">
      Six tools, one mental model: <em>temporarily change the context — who <code>this</code> is,
      what <code>it</code> is called, what the block returns — and Kotlin rewires the scope for you.</em>
    </div>
</LessonLayout>

